Assembler Version 04
====================
Assembled by: Version 03

Features Supported by This Assembler
-------------------------------------
This version keeps the same instruction set as version 03 but does not add
new assembler features compared to version 03. It serves as an intermediate
version that uses version 03's new features (label assignments, byte
selectors, origin setting) in its own source code, establishing patterns
that version 05 will build upon.

Instruction mnemonics (same set as version 03):
  ADC# ADCZ ASLA BCC BCS BEQ BITZ BMI BNE BPL BRK CLC CMPZ,X CMP# CMP,Y
  DEY INCZ INX INY JMP JSR LDA# LDA(),Y LDA,Y LDAZ LDX# LDY# ORAZ RTS
  SBC# SBCZ SEC STA STA(),Y STAZ STAZ,X TYA

DATA directive:
  DATA $XX ...        - emit hex bytes
  DATA "string"       - emit ASCII string with \n and \\ escapes
  DATA $XXXX          - emit 16-bit value (little-endian)
  Mixed: DATA $XX "string" $XXXX on same line

Operand types in instruction arguments:
  $XX                 - 8-bit hex value
  $XXXX               - 16-bit hex value (little-endian)
  "string"            - ASCII string with \n, \\, and \" escapes
  label               - 16-bit label reference (little-endian)
  <label              - low byte of label value (1 byte emitted)
  >label              - high byte of label value (1 byte emitted)

Label features:
  - Labels at column 0 bound to program counter
  - label = $XXXX     - constant assignment (hex value only)
  - * = $XXXX         - set program counter (origin directive)
  - Linear search through label table at $3000
  - Forward references NOT supported (single-pass)
  - Duplicate label detection

Error handling:
  - Error 01: Label not found
  - Error 02: Duplicate label
  - Error 03: Opcode not found
  - Error 04: Expected hex value

Semicolon comments (to end of line).

New in this version (relative to version 03):
- No new assembler features. The assembler capabilities are identical to
  version 03. The significance is that the source code now uses version 03's
  features (label assignments, byte selectors, origin setting), making the
  code more maintainable and preparing for version 05's additions.

Features Used in This Assembler's Source Code
----------------------------------------------
This is the first version whose source code takes full advantage of the
assembler features added in version 03: label constant assignments for
zero-page variables, < byte selectors for zero-page addressing, and > byte
selectors for high-byte extraction.

Source features used:
  - Non-standard instruction mnemonics (from version 03's table)
  - Labels at column 0 for code locations
  - label = $XXXX constant assignments for all zero-page variables
    (TEMP, NEXTCHAR, TABL, TABH, PCL, PCH, HEX1, HEX2, TOKEN)
    and memory addresses (PC_START, LBTAB)
  - Does NOT use * = $XXXX origin directive (a comment says ";  .org $2000"
    but origin is set programmatically via PC_START label in start routine)
  - <label byte selectors for zero-page operands (e.g., INCZ <PCL,
    STAZ <TEMP, LDAZ <NEXTCHAR, LDA(),Y <TABL)
  - >label byte selectors for high-byte extraction (e.g., LDA# >LBTAB,
    LDA# >MNTAB, LDA# >PC_START)
  - <label byte selectors for low-byte extraction (e.g., LDA# <LBTAB,
    LDA# <MNTAB, LDA# <PC_START)
  - Label references as 16-bit operands
  - DATA directive with hex bytes and string literals
  - Character string operands in instructions (e.g., CMP# "\n", CMP# "\\")
  - Hex operands ($XX, $XXXX)
  - Semicolon comments
  - Branch targets as hardcoded hex offsets

New syntax used in source (relative to version 03):
- label = $XXXX constant assignments for zero-page variables and addresses
- < byte selectors for zero-page addressing (e.g., STAZ <TABL instead of STAZ $02)
- > byte selectors for high-byte extraction from labels

Other source changes:
- Named variables instead of raw hex addresses in all operands
